\details{
\subsection{The DAR}{

Every stratum of the catch that has a distribution of \code{distributionType} is a set of
rows, one for each class and variable: a \code{Number} row (\code{distributionvalueType}
\code{"Total"}) and a \code{WeightLive} row (\code{"Mean"}), with the catch of the stratum in
\code{total}. \code{imported} says which strata were allocated: \code{"N"} for a stratum whose
distribution the allocation estimated (\code{sampledOrEstimated == "estimated"}), \code{"Y"} for
a stratum with a sampled one. The two names of the group say how the strata took part:
\itemize{
\item \code{distributionAllocationGroupNameTarget}: the group the stratum was \strong{allocated in},
on the estimated strata (\code{imported = "N"}) and on those only - the \code{allocGroup} of
its distribution.
\item \code{distributionAllocationGroupNameSource}: the group(s) the stratum \strong{feeds}, on the
sampled strata (\code{imported = "Y"}) and on those only - the strata whose sampled
distribution went into the distribution a group estimated. A stratum that feeds
several groups has them all, separated by \code{"; "}. A group's donors are the strata of
its \code{condition_matched_data_list} condition with a sampled distribution of the type
and a catch, and the other rows of the same domains, as \code{\link[=catchAllocationAoL]{catchAllocationAoL()}} picks
them.
\item \code{distributionAllocationSchemeName} is written on the allocated strata only.
}

A group that estimated nothing (all its strata were sampled) is neither a target nor
a source, and has no DAS row.

A distribution several strata share (one \code{domainBiology} for several catch rows) is
shared among them in proportion to their catch: the numbers of each stratum are its
part of the distribution, the mean weights are the distribution's. So the numbers of
the DAR add up to those of \code{\link[=distributionsCombined]{distributionsCombined()}}, which counts a distribution once.
A distribution the allocation table repeats - \code{\link[=catchAllocationAoL]{catchAllocationAoL()}} writes a sampled
distribution once for every catch row of its domain - is counted once. Within one
stratum of the format (which has no \code{metier6}) the numbers of its rows add up, and
their mean weights are averaged, weighted by the numbers. The sampling - \code{PSU},
\code{numPSUs}, \code{numTrips} and \code{numMeasurements} - is that of the distributions, counted
once for each, as in \code{\link[=distributionsCombined]{distributionsCombined()}}; the allocation leaves it empty on the
estimated strata. \code{variance} is left empty. \code{vesselFlagCountry} is the ISO 3166 code,
as in \code{\link[=catchAllocation]{catchAllocation()}}.

The numbers of the estimated strata are checked against their donors: in
\code{\link[=catchAllocationAoL]{catchAllocationAoL()}} the numbers of a stratum are the pooled numbers of the donors
times the catch of the stratum over the catch of the donors. A group whose estimated
numbers are not that is warned about - the conditions are not those of the
allocation, or the allocation counted a donor distribution more than once (a domain
that several donor catch rows share).
}

\subsection{The DAS}{

One row for each group that estimated a distribution: \code{averagingMethod} \code{"Weighted"}
and \code{weightingParameter} the variable of the catch (\code{"WeightLive"}). The numbers of
a stratum are the donors' numbers per unit of catch, the donors weighted by their
catch; the mean weights are averaged by the numbers at each class.
}

\subsection{The header}{

\code{workingGroupYear}, \code{version}, \code{versionDescription}, \code{calendarYear} and
\code{startDateYear} are those of \code{\link[=catchCombined]{catchCombined()}} - give them the same values.
\code{distributionAllocationSchemeName} is the name of the scheme the distributions were
allocated by; it is the \code{versionDescription} when not given.

The field names are the format's own, \code{attibuteValue} (sic) among them: the DAR
names the variable of the catch \code{totalvariableType} and \code{totalvariableUnit}, and
that of the distribution \code{distributionvariableType}, \code{variableUnit} and
\code{distributionvalueType}.
}
